<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Chupanids</h1><p class="st-meta">1335 CE – 1357 CE · capital Tabriz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mongol family of the Suldus clan that dominated north-western Iran after the Ilkhanate collapsed, ruling Tabriz through puppet khans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chupan">Amir Chupan</a> was the most powerful commander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-bahadur-khan">Abu Sa'id</a> until Abu Sa'id had him killed in 1327. After Abu Sa'id's death in 1335, Chupan's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-kuchak">Hasan Kuchak</a> ('Little Hasan') seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and set up puppet khans, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-beg">Sati Beg</a> and Sulayman Khan, to legitimise his rule.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chupanids fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jalayirids">Jalayirids</a> for control of the Ilkhanid heartland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-kuchak">Hasan Kuchak</a> was murdered by his wife in 1343, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ashraf">Malik Ashraf</a> ruled Tabriz with notorious cruelty and extortion, causing many scholars and merchants to flee. Their rule was military, relying on Mongol and Turkish troops, with little stable administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1357 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a>, khan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> at the invitation of exiles, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ashraf">Malik Ashraf</a> and executed him, ending the dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> soon passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jalayirids">Jalayirids</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chupanids.jpg" alt="Chupanids" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Arab League at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chupanid_-_Jalayerid_dynasty_1337%E2%80%931432_ad.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1335 CE – 1357 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tabriz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasan Kuchak, Malik Ashraf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chobanids, Chupanid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1346&amp;focus=chupanids" aria-label="Chupanids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5833 809 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chupanids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6153 1065L6134 1224M6153 1065L6352 637M6153 1065L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6352 637h0M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tabriz. <a href="/atlas?year=1346&amp;focus=chupanids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
